  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs` `impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced `{cite}` on an explicit function with a body. The commentary is in fenced citation commentary, as the built-in requires. The explanation is substantive: the `Graph` trait requires the callback, and this storage-only fixture treats any call to it as an unwanted graph access.\n- **Agreement with the source:** The function body is a guard, `panic!(\"storage operations must not discover graph edges\")`. The storage tests don't call it. The `StoredGraph` `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` methods delegate only through `node_path`, which `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` exercises. `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors` uses `StoredReview`, whose `execute` does no graph traversal. So the function is meant to stay unexecuted, which is the zero-execution case the exception excuses.\n- **Other fixtures:** The claim that other fixtures exercise edge discovery holds. `tests/agent.rs`, `tests/contracts.rs` and `tests/validation.rs` each have real `Graph::outgoing_edges` implementations with edge-returning behavior.\n- **No redundant citation:** The function has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to `habeas:coverage`, so the citation is not redundant.\n\nWhether an accepted independent assessment permits the exception is outside what this review can show.",
